<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="45–55 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson 5</p>
  <h1>Ability and permission with <em>can</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Say what someone is able to do, what is possible now, and what a person or rule allows: <span class="ex">I can drive</span>, <span class="ex">She can’t come today</span>, and <span class="ex">Can I open the window?</span></p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">45–55 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">ability, present possibility, and permission</span>
    <span class="tag">friendly requests</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min optional listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Meet one word with three jobs.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Use can + base verb, always.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Make questions, negatives, and short answers.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Talk about your real skills and rules.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><span class="ex">Can</span> presents an action as possible now. A skill can make it possible, a situation can make it possible, or a person or rule can allow it. In a question to another person, <span class="ex">can</span> can also ask for help.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched has got to the singular subject, used Do for a present-simple question, and introduced one place with There is.">
    <h3>Three quick checks from earlier lessons</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Answer from memory, then check. Say each complete sentence aloud.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="has">has</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="have">have</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Do">Do</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Does">Does</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="There is">There is</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="There are">There are</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The fictional family profile names one sister. My sister <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="has" data-hint="My sister is one third-person subject, so use has before got." data-fix="My sister has got two children." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> got two children. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(have got)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The schedule question asks about your regular weekend routine: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Do" data-hint="A present-simple question with you begins with Do." data-fix="Do you work on weekends?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> you work on weekends? &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(present simple)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>A neighborhood guide introduces one place: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="There is" data-hint="One new coffee shop takes the singular introduction There is." data-fix="There is a coffee shop on this fictional street." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> a coffee shop on this fictional street. &nbsp;<span class="contraction">(there is / there are)</span></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice before the rule</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>One driver, one hot room</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">"I can drive, but I can’t park downtown. Can I open the window?"</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which part talks about a skill?</li>
      <li>Find the negative form.</li>
      <li>Which sentence asks for permission?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. Can marks a possible skill or allowed action, can’t marks an unavailable action, and Can at the front asks permission.">
    <h3>Discovery check: choose the pattern</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Read the whole sentence first. The meaning tells you which form fits.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="can">can</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="can’t">can’t</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Can">Can</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The speaker describes a learned skill: I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="can" data-hint="Going to the pool every week supports a positive swimming ability." data-fix="I can swim, so I go to the pool every week." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> swim, so I go to the pool every week.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The fictional health note says night driving is not safe for Leo: He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="can’t" data-hint="The note makes night driving unavailable, so use the negative can’t." data-fix="He can’t drive at night because his eyes get tired." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> drive at night because his eyes get tired.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The room is hot, and the learner asks the host for permission: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Can" data-hint="A permission question begins Can I followed by the base verb." data-fix="Can I open the window?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> I open the window?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The fictional team profile lists two language skills: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="can" data-hint="The profile confirms a positive ability, so use can." data-fix="She can speak Spanish and English, so she translates for the team." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> speak Spanish and English, so she translates for the team.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The music is very loud, so hearing the caller is not possible now: We <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="can’t" data-hint="The loud music blocks the action in this situation, so use can’t." data-fix="We can’t hear you because the music is very loud." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> hear you because the music is very loud.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Everyone has arrived, and the chair asks the group for permission to begin: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Can" data-hint="The chair asks whether the group allows the action, so begin Can we." data-fix="Can we start the meeting now?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> we start the meeting now?</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>The system: one form for every subject</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>With <span class="ex">can</span>, every subject uses the same pattern. The action verb stays in its base form.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>form</th><th>pattern</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>positive</b></td><td>subject + can + base verb</td><td>She can help.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>negative</b></td><td>subject + can’t + base verb</td><td>She can’t stay.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>question</b></td><td>Can + subject + base verb?</td><td>Can she help?</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">Short answers: <span class="ex">Yes, I can. / No, she can’t.</span> Do not repeat the action verb in a short answer.</p>
  <p class="note-line">The key rule: <span class="ex">can</span> + base verb for <strong>every</strong> subject. <span class="ex">I can drive, she can drive, they can drive.</span> No <span class="ex">-s</span>, no <span class="ex">do / does</span>, ever.</p>

  <h2>Get to the root: one idea, four everyday uses</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Can</span> is a <strong>modal verb</strong>. A modal does not name the main action. It adds the speaker’s view of that action. With <span class="ex">can</span>, the speaker presents the action as possible now. The context explains why it is possible.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>meaning</th><th>example</th><th>what it says</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>ability</b></td><td>I can swim.</td><td>A skill makes the action possible.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>present possibility</b></td><td>I can talk now.</td><td>The situation makes the action possible now.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>permission</b></td><td>You can leave early. Can I open the window?</td><td>A rule or a person allows it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>request</b></td><td>Can you help me?</td><td>You ask someone to do something for you.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Ability or this situation?</h3>
  <p>A negative with <span class="ex">can’t</span> does not always mean “I do not have the skill.” Compare <span class="ex">I can’t drive</span> in a driving lesson with <span class="ex">I can’t drive you today because my car is at the repair shop</span>. The first is about general ability. The second is about what the present situation allows.</p>

  <h3>Context decides the meaning</h3>
  <p>The words alone do not always tell you which meaning is active. The situation does.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>sentence</th><th>likely situation</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Can you drive?</td><td>choosing a driver for a trip</td><td>Do you have the ability?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Can you drive the company car?</td><td>asking about a workplace rule</td><td>Do you have permission?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Can you drive me to the airport?</td><td>asking a friend for a favor</td><td>Will you do it for me?</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Can is the moving part</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">She can drive. → Can she drive? → She can’t drive.</span></p>
    <p>Only <span class="ex">can</span> moves to the front or becomes negative. The base verb <span class="ex">drive</span> never changes. This is why <span class="ex">Does she can drive?</span> mixes two different systems and is not the target form.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Keep the time boundary clear</h3>
    <p>This lesson uses <span class="ex">can</span> for ability, permission, and possibility that are true now or generally. Do not combine present <span class="ex">can</span> with a finished past time. Say <span class="ex">I could swim when I was five</span>, not <span class="ex">I can swim when I was five</span>. Later lessons develop past <span class="ex">could</span> and future <span class="ex">be able to</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. The stated skill, present obstacle, permission rule, or requested action makes each can chunk defensible.">
    <h3>Drill 1: ability, permission, or request</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Read the whole situation first. Then choose the chunk that fits.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="can">can</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="can’t">can’t</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Can I">Can I</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Can you">Can you</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The fictional staff profile says my brother repairs computers at work. My brother <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="can" data-hint="His IT work provides evidence of a positive skill." data-fix="My brother can fix computers because he works in IT." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> fix computers because he works in IT.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>I cannot lift the boxes alone, so I make a friendly request: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Can you" data-hint="A request for another person’s action begins Can you." data-fix="Can you help me with these boxes, please?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> help me with these boxes, please?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>A fictional employee has an urgent family responsibility today. Sorry, I <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="can’t" data-hint="The urgent responsibility makes coming unavailable today, so use can’t." data-fix="Sorry, I can’t come to the office today." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> come to the office today.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>I need the teacher’s permission before changing the topic: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Can I" data-hint="A permission question about your own action begins Can I." data-fix="Can I ask a question about the schedule?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> ask a question about the schedule?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The posted school rule allows phones only during the break. Students <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="can" data-hint="The rule allows the action during the break, so use positive can." data-fix="Students can use their phones during the break, but not in class." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> use their phones during the break, but not in class.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The live parking map shows every downtown space is full. She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="can’t" data-hint="The full streets make parking impossible in this situation; this is not a statement about her skill." data-fix="She can’t find a parking spot downtown this morning." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> find a parking spot downtown this morning.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">7</span><span>I am in a meeting and ask for a later action: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Can you" data-hint="You want the other person to call, so use the request frame Can you." data-fix="Can you call me back after lunch?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> call me back after lunch?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">8</span><span>The fictional travel profile confirms a useful family skill. My parents <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="can" data-hint="The profile confirms positive language ability, so use can." data-fix="My parents can speak a little English, so the group travels easily." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> speak a little English, so the group travels easily.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Permission, requests, and politeness</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Can I...?</span> is normal and friendly for everyday permission. <span class="ex">Can you...?</span> is often a request, not a question about skill. A calm voice, a reason when useful, and <span class="ex">please</span> can make the request warmer: <span class="ex">Can you open the window, please?</span></p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>purpose</th><th>question</th><th>natural response</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>ask permission</b></td><td>Can I sit here?</td><td>Sure. / Sorry, this seat is taken.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>make a request</b></td><td>Can you send the file, please?</td><td>Of course. / Sorry, I can’t right now.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>ask about ability</b></td><td>Can you use this program?</td><td>Yes, I can. / No, I can’t.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">The grammar can be identical while the social action changes. Listen to the situation before you answer. <span class="ex">Yes, I can</span> answers an ability question, but <span class="ex">Sure</span> more naturally accepts a request.</p>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: can or can’t in U.S. English?</h3>
    <p>In an ordinary positive statement, <span class="ex">can</span> is often weak and quick, like <span class="ex">kuhn</span>, while the action carries the main beat: <span class="ex">I can <strong>SWIM</strong></span>. When <span class="ex">can</span> is contrasted or placed at the end, it is strong: <span class="ex">I <strong>CAN</strong> swim</span> and <span class="ex">Yes, I <strong>CAN</strong></span>.</p>
    <p>Negative <span class="ex">can’t</span> is normally stressed: <span class="ex">I <strong>CAN’T</strong> swim</span>. In American speech, the final <span class="ex">t</span> may be light or unreleased, so do not depend on that sound alone. Stress, the vowel, the following words, and the situation all help the listener. If the contrast matters, repeat the full idea clearly: <span class="ex">I can come Friday, but I can’t come Thursday.</span></p>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read can meaning and spoken-contrast listening check">
    <h2>Listen: what does <em>can</em> do here?</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p>Your teacher reads each complete fictional context once at a natural speed and once more slowly. Listen for the situation, the sentence stress, and whether <span class="ex">can</span> expresses ability, present possibility, permission, or a request. Open the script only after checking.</p>
    <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You used the complete situation and spoken contrast to distinguish ability, present possibility, permission, and a friendly request.">
      <h3>Tutor-read listening check</h3>
      <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Can meaning choices">
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="ability">ability</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="present possibility">present possibility</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="permission question">permission question</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="permission given">permission given</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="request">request</button>
      </div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Clip 1 mainly expresses <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="ability" data-hint="The profile describes a learned workplace skill that is generally true." data-fix="Mina can repair bicycles. This is ability." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Clip 2 mainly expresses <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="present possibility" data-hint="The sentence says the speaker has time now, not that talking is a learned skill." data-fix="I can talk now because my meeting is over. This is present possibility." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Clip 3 is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="permission question" data-hint="The visitor asks whether their own action is allowed." data-fix="Can I charge my phone here? This is a permission question." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Clip 4 expresses <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="permission given" data-hint="The host tells a visitor that an action is allowed." data-fix="You can use the desk by the window. This gives permission." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Clip 5 is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="request" data-hint="The speaker wants another person to perform the action." data-fix="Can you speak more slowly, please? This is a request." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Clip 6 mainly expresses <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="present possibility" data-hint="The train schedule blocks the action today; the sentence does not question anyone’s travel skill." data-fix="We can’t take the 6:00 train because it is canceled. This is present impossibility." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
      <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
    </div>
    <details class="thinking-card">
      <summary>Open the teacher script after checking</summary>
      <ol>
        <li><span class="ex">Mina can repair bicycles. She works at a neighborhood bike shop.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">My meeting is over, so I can talk now.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">My battery is low. Can I charge my phone here?</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">You can use the desk by the window. Nobody is using it.</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">I understand the topic, but the pace is fast. Can you speak more slowly, please?</span></li>
        <li><span class="ex">The 6:00 train is canceled, so we can’t take it today.</span></li>
      </ol>
      <p>Read the ordinary positive <span class="ex">can</span> weakly and the negative <span class="ex">can’t</span> with a clear beat. Keep the surrounding context natural.</p>
    </details>
  </section>

  <div class="practice" data-answer-drill data-success-message="Correct. Each question begins with Can, and each one-word short answer keeps can or can’t without repeating the action verb.">
    <h3>Drill 2: type the question word or short answer</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Questions start with <b>Can</b>. Short answers end with <b>can</b> or <b>can’t</b>, never the action verb.</p>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>A manager asks about your general availability: <input class="answer-input" data-answer="Can" data-hint="A can question moves Can before the subject you." data-fix="Can you work on Saturdays?" aria-label="Question starter before you work on Saturdays" /> you work on Saturdays?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>A travel profile asks about your sister’s ability: <input class="answer-input" data-answer="Can" data-hint="Ability questions also begin with Can before the subject." data-fix="Can your sister drive?" aria-label="Question starter before your sister drive" /> your sister drive?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The profile says she does not have that skill. “Can your sister drive?” “No, she <input class="answer-input" data-answer="can’t" data-hint="The prompt requires one word, so keep can and contract not." data-fix="No, she can’t." aria-label="One-word negative short answer after No she" />.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The venue is available to the group. “Can we meet here?” “Yes, we <input class="answer-input" data-answer="can" data-hint="A positive short answer keeps can and omits meet here." data-fix="Yes, we can." aria-label="One-word positive short answer after Yes we" />.”</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>A fictional support form asks about two people’s computer ability: <input class="answer-input" data-answer="Can" data-hint="Move Can before the plural subject your parents." data-fix="Can your parents use a computer?" aria-label="Question starter before your parents use a computer" /> your parents use a computer?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The form records a negative answer. “Can they use a computer?” “No, they <input class="answer-input" data-answer="can’t" data-hint="The prompt requires one word, so use the negative short-answer form can’t." data-fix="No, they can’t." aria-label="One-word negative short answer after No they" />.”</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions">
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-check-answers>Check</button>
      <button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-answers>Reset</button>
    </div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Read and choose</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>A job ad in the café window</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">"Weekend barista wanted. You can choose your hours, and you can learn everything on the job. Staff can’t park behind the café, but you can leave a bike in the yard. Can you start in June? Ask for Dana at the counter."</span></p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You located the available choice, the parking restriction, and the requested June start date in the fictional job ad.">
    <h3>Drill 3: read, then choose</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="their hours" data-hint="The first sentence after the job title names what staff may choose." data-fix="New staff can choose their hours.">
        <p class="quiz-question">1. What can new staff choose?</p>
