  </div>

  <h2>Notice and repair errors</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="cans" data-hint="A modal has one form for every subject, including she." data-fix="She can swim very well." data-why="Can never changes. It has no -s form, even after she, he, or it.">
    <h3>Drill 6: one form for every subject</h3>
    <p>Tap the word that is wrong.</p>
    <div class="spot-options">
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="she">She</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="cans">cans</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="swim">swim</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="very well">very well.</button>
    </div>
    <p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="do" data-hint="Can forms its own question by moving before you, so one question helper is extra." data-fix="Can you drive?" data-why="Can makes its own questions. Move can in front of the subject and drop do.">
    <h3>Drill 7: can asks its own questions</h3>
    <p>Tap the word that is wrong.</p>
    <div class="spot-options">
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="do">Do</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="you">you</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="can">can</button>
      <button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="drive">drive?</button>
    </div>
    <p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="micro-drill" data-transform>
    <h3>Drill 8: say it, then reveal</h3>
    <p>Read the cue, say the full sentence aloud, then reveal only after you answer.</p>
    <div class="transform-list">
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>1</b> I can drive. Change to she.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>She can drive.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>2</b> They can come on Friday. Make it negative.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>They can’t come on Friday.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>3</b> You want to use the meeting room. Ask for permission.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Can I use the meeting room?</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>4</b> You need help with two heavy bags. Ask a friend politely.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Can you help me with these bags, please?</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>5</b> Can he swim? Answer yes.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Yes, he can.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item>
        <p class="transform-cue"><b>6</b> Can they stay late tonight? Answer no.</p>
        <button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button>
        <p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>No, they can’t.</strong></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Use it in real life</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to disclose your real skills, health, family, workplace, schedule, location, or access needs. Choose a fictional profile, a public role, or a teacher-provided card whenever you prefer.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Fictional skills card:</b> Choose a character such as a café worker, neighbor, traveler, or new employee. Give the character three abilities and one skill they do not have yet. <span class="ex">Ari can make coffee and use the register, but he can’t repair the machine yet.</span></div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Situation change:</b> Give the character one action that is generally possible but unavailable today. <span class="ex">She can drive, but she can’t drive today because the car is at the repair shop.</span></div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Information desk:</b> Ask two permission questions and make two requests. The teacher responds naturally with <span class="ex">Sure</span>, <span class="ex">Of course</span>, or a short reason for saying no.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Meaning switch:</b> Use the same beginning, <span class="ex">Can you use the printer...?</span>, once to ask about ability and once to make a request. Add enough context to make each meaning clear.</div>

  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Final production: run a fictional community-center help desk</h3>
    <p>Create a 45–60 second exchange between a visitor and a staff member. Include all five pieces of evidence:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>one general ability with <span class="ex">can</span>;</li>
      <li>one present obstacle with <span class="ex">can’t</span> and a reason;</li>
      <li>one <span class="ex">Can I...?</span> permission question;</li>
      <li>one <span class="ex">Can you...?</span> request with a natural response;</li>
      <li>one clear contrast in which weak <span class="ex">can</span> and stressed <span class="ex">can’t</span> are easy to hear.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The listener names the meaning of each <span class="ex">can</span> form and asks one follow-up question. Revise any line whose context permits more than one reading.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Reflect</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Say the system back</h3>
    <p>Complete this aloud: “After <span class="ex">can</span>, I always use the ______ verb. <span class="ex">Can</span> is the same for ______ subject. For a question, ______ moves in front of the subject. The negative is ______.”</p>
    <p>Then use a real or fictional context to say one general ability, one present obstacle, one permission question, and one friendly request.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Great. You can talk about ability, ask permission, and make polite requests with can.">
    <h3>Final quiz: can forms and meaning</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="can run" data-hint="After can, use the unchanged base verb and do not add -s to either word." data-fix="The athletics profile says he can run very fast.">
